@namespace PanelGallery.UiTests
@inherits UiTestPage

@using Sandbox;
@using Sandbox.UI;

@attribute [Title( "About" )]
@attribute [Description( "What this section is for and how to add to it" )]
@attribute [Icon( "help" )]
@attribute [Order( 0 )]

<root>

    <h2>Reading a case</h2>

    <p>
        Every case prints the CSS that styled it. It's the same string, not a copy, so you can paste
        it into a browser and the two should match at 1:1. Sizes and fills that aren't the point of
        the test are left out of the caption.
    </p>

    <div class="cases">
        <UiTestCase Css="border-radius: 12px 0" Note="A note says what to look for when the CSS doesn't." />
    </div>

    <h2>This window</h2>

    <p>
        Scale 1, not desktop scaling, so a CSS pixel is a screen pixel. Leave that alone - it's why
        a screenshot from here can be diffed against a browser.
    </p>

    <p>
        This window draws with no post processing, so HDR colours clamp instead of glowing. The HDR
        page still proves the values parse and blend - seeing the glow needs a scene with bloom.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding a page</h2>

    <p>
        Derive a razor panel from UiTestPage in this folder. It shows up in the list on its own -
        Title, Description, Icon and Order place it. Cases go in a "cases" div. Class a case
        "checkered" for alpha, "dark" for glows, "roomy" when it draws outside its box, "wide" for
        a long caption.
    </p>

    <p>
        Keep a case that shows a bug, and say so in its note. Don't keep one that throws - an
        exception while the tree builds repeats every frame and buries the console.
    </p>

    <p>
        Watch what you name a const in a page: a page is a Panel, so Box, Style, Parent and the
        rest of Panel's members are taken, and hiding one is a build error, not a warning.
    </p>

</root>
